{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 (rebar-debug) COMPLETE — root cause confirmed with a cited mechanism.\n\nMECHANISM: This host's agent harness injects git config via environment: GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=2, GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.bareRepository, GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=explicit (plus credential.interactive=never). Under safe.bareRepository=explicit (git >= 2.38 security policy), git refuses IMPLICIT discovery of a bare repository: any 'git -C <bare.git> <cmd>' exits 128 with \"fatal: cannot use bare repository '<path>' (safe.bareRepository is 'explicit')\". Explicit naming via 'git --git-dir <bare.git>' is always permitted, as are push/fetch/clone against the bare path.\n\nWHY THIS TEST: tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_ref_lock.py's _git() helper (line ~57) runs ['git', '-C', str(repo), ...]; test_ac0_blob_ref_roundtrips_through_remote passes the BARE fixture repo to it (cat-file -t at line 215, rev-parse at line 217). The push succeeds (the object IS in the bare remote — earlier 'object absent' diagnoses were wrong); the verification command itself is refused. Reproduced deterministically in a scratch dir: 'git -C bare.git cat-file -t <oid>' → 128 with the exact fatal above; 'git --git-dir bare.git cat-file -t <oid>' → 'blob', exit 0; the same oid resolves via rev-parse. This also explains why prior sessions saw it as env-specific: only hosts whose harness injects the explicit policy fail.\n\nDEFECT CLASSIFICATION: a REAL test-portability defect, not purely environmental. The repo's own contract says bare-repo fixtures must survive safe.bareRepository=explicit: tests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py enforces exactly this policy for other fixtures, and prior art (tests/_git_upkeep.py::_git, test_concurrency_regression.py::_bare_git) already uses 'git --git-dir' for bare remotes. test_ref_lock.py predates/misses that pattern.\n\nFIX (Phase 2, strict RED→GREEN): route bare-repo git invocations in test_ref_lock.py through explicit --git-dir naming (matching the _git_upkeep prior art) so the test passes under both the default and the explicit safe.bareRepository policy. RED = test fails under injected explicit policy (reproduced now); GREEN = passes under both explicit and devnull-global policies, 5x locally, full reconciler suite green. Production _ref_lock code is NOT implicated — it never runs git inside a bare repo via -C."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787349386081296001,"uuid":"06210a5e-2c71-4eae-aa84-26d66eeca0c7"}